 ingredients needed to describe the  balance  in this case of velocity and vorticity  in this field and  this is kind of generally the philosophy  we're going to take  is that from this data we don't just  want a model  that fits that data there's tons of  machine learning algorithms that will  give  you accurate long-term forecasts of  these dynamical systems  but a lot of those are kind of opaque or  black box models  we really put a premium on getting  equations dynamical systems models that  are sparse  that have as few degrees of freedom  possible that describe  the behavior so that's kind of what  we're looking for here  and this really rests on the idea that  out of all of the possible dynamical  systems that could have described  this lorentz system or this fluid flow  these are actually quite simple the  right hand sides or these differential  equations  only have a very few terms in the  equations the lorentz equations have  seven terms  and likewise the navier-stokes equations  are quite simple out of all the possible  differential equation terms that could  describe them these are both relatively  sparse so that's what we mean by the  sparse identification  of nonlinear dynamics and there's a  third interpretation that we'll talk  about also which is  even if i have a spatial temporal system  a high dimensional system  like this fluid flow i think when i  simulate this i need  approximately a hundred thousand degrees  of freedom in the computer  um you can discover a partial  differential equation  but you might also want to represent  this as a low dimensional combination of  a few  basis modes so oftentimes you can take  these  uh spatial temporal fields like a fluid  flow  and decompose it into a few key  variables in this case this combination  of  three modes with amplitudes x y and z  and you can derive a sparse differential  equation model  in terms of those mode amplitudes and so  this is a classic model from the  literature by barrett noak and  