 want  sparsity why do we care  about having minimalistic simplistic  models  and you've heard me say this before i'm  going to say it again  it's very important kind of perspective  in machine learning  is that as we start as we continue to  design  and develop and discover models from  data  that's what machine learning is as we  discover our models from data  of dynamical systems we have to put a  premium on models that are interpretable  and generalizable if we ever want to  have  these models be useful for safety  critical control systems for autopilots  for autonomy for robotic systems  in the loop with humans then we need our  models to be interpretable we need to  understand  what they mean and they need to  generalize outside  of the training data where we train them  instead of defining these two  uh words what i'm going to do is give  you an example  so i think that the ultimate  interpretable and generalizable model  is newton's f equals ma so  it's interpretable in the sense that it  has three terms  f m and a and we can write these down  for a problem of interest we can analyze  explain interpret they have units  and it's generalizable in the sense that  newton can learn  f equals ma from apples falling on earth  that's how the story goes  and this law f equals ma is still valid  when we design  space missions to the moon that's  the ultimate generalizable model so i  guarantee  that you could take movies of apples  falling data from apple's falling and  you could train a deep neural network  to make new movies of apples falling or  to predict the motion of those apples  but that deep neural network is not  going to be useful for space mission  design  unless you take a big step back and  guarantee that you're enforcing  interpretable and generalizable  models so that's what we want we want  models that are more like f equals ma  and less like these big opaque neural  networks  and this is a principle in physical  modeling that's been around for  for t