w that there are governing equations  but we don't know what they are we just  measure x y and z the state of the  system  in time so every row is a measurement in  time  and we assume that we can compute the  derivatives x dot y dot and z dot  now that's a big assumption and we're  going to relax that assumption in a  minute  but assume you have access to  measurements of the data  in time now the simplest thing that you  could try  would be to model this dynamical system  using a linear model  or a best fit 3x3 a matrix that best  predicts the time derivatives  from the measurement of the state now  and this is what the dynamic mode  decomposition or dmd  would do is essentially it would give  you a best fit three by three model  that would best propagate that system  forward in time  but we know that a three by three linear  model is not going to capture the rich  dynamics of this lorentz system  a simple a matrix has a single fixed  point at the origin  it can't describe the multiple fixed  points of the laurent system the  unstable periodic orbits  or the uh the rich chaotic dynamics that  we observe  so instead what the cindy algorithm does  is it augments the right-hand side of  possible model  terms to include nonlinear terms in  addition to the state itself  so in this case for example in addition  to x y and z  we add terms x squared x y y  squared and all the way up in this case  to fifth order polynomials  so we augment our library to include  more  terms that could be used to describe the  left-hand side the x dot the y-dot and  the z-dot  and notice that all of these uh gray  columns in this theta library  can be computed from the data that i  measured in x y and z  if i have x i can compute x squared if i  have x and y  i can compute x times y and so on so  this is a computable matrix  and now the entire objective of the  cindy algorithm  is to try to find the fewest terms in  this  library of candidate dynamics that  describe the x dot equation  that describe the y dot measurements and  that describe the z dot  measurements okay so for example i'm  trying to find the fewest columns of  theta that add up to equal x dot  that add up to equal y dot and then add  up to equal z dot  now 20 years ago this would have been a  combinatorial brute force search  out of all possible sparse combinations  of columns to find the best fit to x dot  y dot and z dot  now that's a commodity algorithm in  optimization  there are dozens of sparse optimization  algorithms that will give you the  sparsest  subset of theta that adds up to x dot y  dot and z  dot we have our own favorite that i'll  tell you about later but there's lots of  them like the lasso  hard thresholding and so on and so forth  and so when you do this sparse  optimization in this library  you essentially learn the structure of  your dynamical system  and the parameters of what terms are  active  so you learn that the x dot equation is  linear in x and y  which is actually true in the lorentz  equations the y dot equation has a  linear term in  x and y and a nonlinear x z term  and the z dot equations have a z and an  x y  term and in fact that is exactly what  the true lorentz equations are governed  by  and we learn that purely from  measurement data of our system  so this is something that is true in  almost every  system we've looked at where we knew the  answer  is that by imposing this sparsity  condition by trying to find the sparsest  model possible  that that explains the observed data we  tend to discover the actual  true dynamics that generated the data in  the first place  and that's very promising because if we  can rediscover  dynamics that we know using the sparse  algorithm  then hopefully we can discover new  dynamical systems that we don't know  and still have them be interpretable and  generalizable  like these models okay good  now you can do this for ordinary  differential equations like i've shown  you  i'm going to show you all of the  chal